<Layout title="Codex monitor"><div class="wrap"><header class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">A desk companion built from small diagnostics</p><h1>Your agents.<br />At a glance.</h1><p class="lede">A landscape dashboard for Codex usage and agent activity over Wi-Fi, plus touchscreen Codex Micro controls and a microphone over USB.</p></header><div class="article-grid"><article class="prose"><figure><img class="screen" src={`${base}/docs/images/codex-monitor-screen.png`} width="320" height="240" alt="Actual monitor display showing live usage and a working agent" /><figcaption>Actual FNK0104B LCD readback, 4 October 2026. This is a captured moment, not a live dashboard.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>What you can do</h2><ul><li>See remaining five-hour and weekly usage with reset countdowns.</li><li>See animated agent activity and select an agent for a message.</li><li>Say “Hi ESP” for the five local commands shown in the listening guide.</li><li>Record a message by touch or voice, then send it through your bridge to a configured transcription service.</li><li>Adjust alert volume, idle screen timeout and USB Micro layout through Web Bluetooth.</li><li>Configure Wi-Fi with the board's on-screen setup code.</li></ul><p>Approvals stay in the Codex client. Visibility of threads running in a separate Codex process depends on what the bridge's app-server connection can observe.</p>
<h2 id="usb-micro">Use Codex Micro over USB HID</h2>
<p>The monitor includes an unofficial Codex Micro compatibility mode. Connect the board with a USB data cable to the computer running Codex Desktop and let Desktop discover it. USB power alone does not activate Micro: the firmware waits for a discovery/status exchange. The screen then shows Desktop slots and controls. Wi-Fi can remain connected, but USB Micro does not require a private bridge host/key.</p>
<p>If Desktop runs on Windows, leave USB owned by Windows. An active USB/IP attachment to WSL gives WSL the whole device and prevents Windows Desktop from using its HID controls. On macOS, allow Input Monitoring if Desktop requests it.</p>
<figure><img class="screen" src={`${base}/docs/images/usb-micro-compact-preview.png`} width="320" height="240" loading="lazy" alt="Host preview of the USB Micro layout with three agent slots, four directions, Hold to talk, Voice and Send" /><figcaption>Compact USB Micro layout · host-rendered sample, 10 October 2026.</figcaption></figure>
<h3>Choose the controls on the board</h3>
<ol><li>Keep the board powered and open <a href={`${base}/setup.html#setup`}>Device setup → Monitor settings</a> in Chrome or Edge.</li><li>Click <strong>Connect to monitor</strong> and select <strong>FNK0104B-MONITOR</strong>. USB HID can remain connected; no OS Bluetooth pairing is needed.</li><li>Choose <strong>3 slots + directions</strong> (the default) or <strong>6 agent slots</strong>, then click <strong>Save board settings</strong>. The layout applies without reboot and is saved on the board. Older firmware needs an update if the selector is disabled.</li></ol>
<p>The tiles are stable Desktop positions: Agent 1–3 send AG00–AG02; the six-slot layout also exposes AG03–AG05. They do not count running agents. Lighting comes from Desktop and does not establish thread names or reliable activity meanings.</p>
<p>Tap the board's <strong>Micro header indicator</strong> to switch between agent controls and six command buttons. Tap it again to return. <strong>Hold to talk</strong>, optional <strong>Voice</strong> and <strong>Send</strong> remain below. Screen labels stay fixed after a Desktop remap, so remember your assignments.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Board command</th><th>Desktop key</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fast</td><td>ACT06</td></tr><tr><td>Approve</td><td>ACT07</td></tr><tr><td>Reject</td><td>ACT08</td></tr><tr><td>Fork</td><td>ACT09</td></tr><tr><td>Hold to talk / Mic</td><td>ACT10</td></tr><tr><td>Voice (optional)</td><td>ACT11</td></tr><tr><td>Send</td><td>ACT12</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Direction buttons send joystick movement and a neutral release, rather than keyboard arrow key presses. Configure them as analog-stick directions in Desktop. Direction interpretation and remapping still require a physical check in your Desktop version.</p>
<h3 id="custom-actions">Map buttons to custom actions in Codex Desktop</h3>
<ol><li>After discovery, open <strong>Settings → Codex Micro</strong>.</li><li>For agent buttons, choose <strong>Custom assignments</strong> and assign a chat, shortcut or enabled skill to the matching slot.</li><li>For command buttons, select the corresponding key in the <strong>Layout</strong> preview and choose its action. For example, assign Fork to an enabled skill.</li><li>For arrows, edit the <strong>analog stick</strong> direction assignments. For example, assign Right to an enabled skill.</li><li>Try each assignment on the board. Use <strong>Reset layout</strong> to restore command and stick defaults.</li></ol>
<p>These host settings are described in the <a href="https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/features/codex-micro">official Codex Micro guide</a>, which currently calls the host ChatGPT desktop. It documents the retail device; this board's compatibility is unofficial.</p>
<h3>Hold to talk by default; Voice when you want it</h3>
<p>Monitor 0.6.3 starts with outgoing USB audio off. Select <strong>Microphone (TinyUSB UAC1)</strong> in Desktop and keep its first microphone key on <strong>Push to talk</strong>. Hold the board's <strong>Hold to talk</strong> button to speak, release to silence board audio, then review and press Send. Desktop's double-tap dictation does not bypass this board's hold/release gate.</p>
<p>To add continuous conversation, enable <strong>Use separate microphone keys</strong> in Desktop and assign the second switch to <strong>Voice Chat</strong>, if available. Then choose <strong>Hold to talk + Voice toggle</strong> in <a href={`${base}/setup.html#monitor-heading`}>browser setup</a> and save. Tap Voice to open board audio and request a voice chat; tap again to close board audio and toggle the desktop microphone. Hold to request ending the chat; a one-second hold also closes board audio. Turn Voice off before using Hold to talk.</p>
<p>The browser configures board preferences only; it cannot change or verify Desktop mappings. <strong>Mic ON</strong> means the board is sending live audio to a host stream; <strong>Mic OFF</strong> means silence; <strong>Waiting</strong> means no host stream is open. These indicators do not confirm Desktop recording or session state. Sampling continues locally. Saving settings, rebooting or reconnecting returns board audio to off.</p>
<figure><img class="screen" src={`${base}/docs/images/usb-micro-voice-preview.png`} width="320" height="240" loading="lazy" alt="Host-rendered optional Voice control with Mic ON for outgoing board audio" /><figcaption>Voice-enabled layout · host preview, 10 October 2026. This illustrates board audio, not a verified desktop voice session.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>The connection, explained</h2><div class="card"><p><strong>Board → Wi-Fi → local Python bridge → signed-in Codex app-server</strong></p><p>Recorded message → bridge → configured transcription service → selected Codex thread.</p><p>Board → USB HID + USB microphone → Desktop Micro controls and host voice.</p><p>Browser → Bluetooth LE → board preferences or Wi-Fi provisioning.</p></div><p>GitHub Pages serves this guide and firmware files. Your computer runs the bridge. The browser is not the status server, and USB is not needed for normal Wi-Fi status after setup.</p>
<h2>Get your own monitor working</h2><ol><li><strong>Prepare the network.</strong> Use 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi that can reach your computer. A monitor with no credentials opens secure provisioning; use <a href={`${base}/setup.html#setup`}>Device setup</a> with its displayed code.</li><li><strong>Configure the private bridge.</strong> Follow the repository setup walkthrough to put your computer's LAN address and a generated shared key in the ignored local header. The public browser image has neither.</li><li><strong>Run the bridge.</strong> Use the signed-in Codex app-server in the same environment. WSL may require LAN forwarding; a Windows Codex socket does not automatically exist inside WSL.</li><li><strong>Build and upload locally.</strong> Use the named <code>codex-monitor</code> PlatformIO environment, including its speech models. Inspect serial at 115200 and check Wi-Fi and application status separately.</li><li><strong>Configure preferences.</strong> Connect to <code>FNK0104B-MONITOR</code> in the browser picker for volume, idle timeout and the three- or six-slot USB Micro layout.</li><li><strong>Add transcription if wanted.</strong> Configure a compatible service behind the bridge. Local screen/status commands already work without it.</li></ol><p><a class="button" href="https://github.com/cmwen/FNK0104B/blob/main/docs/monitor-setup.md">Open the full setup walkthrough ↗</a></p>
<h2>Listening is two interactions</h2><p>“Hi ESP” opens a twelve-second window for “start listening”, “go back”, “show status”, “turn on the screen” and “turn off the screen”. “Show status” holds the quota view; “go back” or a card tap restores agent overview.</p><p>A message capture allows ten seconds to start speaking, approximately five seconds of silence before submission and a thirty-second maximum. Tap to stop early. Recognition pauses during capture/submission; quiet mode and attention tones are coordinated with listening.</p>
<h2>Other screen states</h2><div class="screens"><figure><img src={`${base}/docs/images/monitor-command.png`} width="320" height="240" loading="lazy" alt="Firmware-rendered local command listening guide" /><figcaption>Command listening · host preview with sample data.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${base}/docs/images/monitor-recording.png`} width="320" height="240" loading="lazy" alt="Firmware-rendered message recording panel" /><figcaption>Message recording · host preview with sample data.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${base}/docs/images/monitor-idle.png`} width="320" height="240" loading="lazy" alt="Firmware-rendered idle quota view" /><figcaption>Idle quota view · host preview with sample data.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${base}/docs/images/monitor-offline.png`} width="320" height="240" loading="lazy" alt="Firmware-rendered offline monitor state" /><figcaption>Offline feedback · host preview with sample data.</figcaption></figure></div>
<h2>What has been verified?</h2><p>The current monitor was uploaded with verified image hashes. Serial confirmed saved Wi-Fi reconnection, speech models ready, continuous audio-front-end processing and live Codex SSE. The LCD screenshot above was captured from the board. Previous mock runs exercised error/recovery and usage endpoints.</p><p>Spoken recognition accuracy, meter sensitivity, sustained memory stability, actual long capture and running-agent message interactions remain <strong>UNKNOWN</strong> until exercised. Read the dated evidence before treating a build or a host preview as a physical test.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/cmwen/FNK0104B/blob/main/docs/monitor-readiness.md">Monitor readiness and remaining checks ↗</a></p>
